@startuml read-then-write-race
title Why CP1's Read-Then-MULTI-Write Handoff Loses a Claim

skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam sequenceParticipantBorderColor #888888
skinparam sequenceLifeLineBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam sequenceGroupBorderColor #CCCCCC
skinparam sequenceGroupBodyBackgroundColor #FAFAFA

participant "Position Consumer" as PC #DBEAFE
participant "Redis\nalert-state:{entity_id}" as Redis #FEF9C3
participant "Alert Evaluator" as AE #D1FAE5

PC -> Redis : HGETALL alert-state
Redis --> PC : composite_claim_candidate_id = ""

note right of PC
  Snapshot taken. This value is now
  stale the instant it's read -- nothing
  stops another client from writing
  alert-state before the MULTI below runs.
end note

AE -> Redis : CLAIM (Lua, atomic)\ncandidate_id = "Y"
Redis --> AE : claimed\ncomposite_claim_candidate_id = "Y"

note right of Redis
  alert-state now genuinely holds
  candidate Y's claim.
end note

group MULTI / EXEC -- built from the STALE snapshot above
  PC -> Redis : HSET recent-loss\ncomposite_claim_candidate_id = ""  (* stale *)
  PC -> Redis : DEL alert-state
end
Redis --> PC : done

note over Redis
  recent-loss now exists with
  composite_claim_candidate_id = ""
  Candidate Y's claim, written AFTER
  the HGETALL but BEFORE the MULTI,
  is gone -- lost, not merely delayed.
end note

@enduml
